@startuml Sandbox Environment Setup Sequence

!theme plain
title SABER Docker Sandbox Environment Setup - Current Implementation

actor Agent
participant "SessionManager" as SM
participant "BenchmarkManager" as BM
participant "EpisodeManager" as EM_EP
participant "ExecutionManager" as EM
participant "SandboxEnvironmentManager" as SBMGR
participant "ComposeOrchestrator" as CO
participant "Docker Compose" as DC

== Episode Initialization (Current Flow) ==

Agent -> SM: POST /api/v1/session/{session_id}/episodes?task_id=dvwa_sql_injection
activate SM

SM -> BM: get_task(task_id)
activate BM

BM --> SM: Task (with environment, execution_config, episode_config)
deactivate BM

SM -> EM_EP: start_episode(session_id, task_id, initial_context, task)
activate EM_EP

EM_EP -> EM_EP: create Episode object (state=ACTIVE)
EM_EP -> EM_EP: handle dependency resolution if needed
EM_EP --> SM: Episode
deactivate EM_EP

== Task Configuration and Environment Setup ==

SM -> EM: configure_for_task(episode_id, task, session_id, target_episode_id)
activate EM

note over EM
ExecutionManager configures from task object:
- Initializes SandboxEnvironmentManager if needed
- Creates episode-specific environment immediately
- Configures ExecutorFactory with task config
- Handles network attachment for dependent episodes
end note

EM -> SBMGR: create_episode_environment(episode_id, task.environment, target_episode_id)
activate SBMGR

SBMGR -> CO: new ComposeOrchestrator(logging_config)
activate CO

CO -> CO: resolve compose file path from task.environment
CO -> DC: docker-compose up -d with episode-specific config
activate DC

DC -> DC: Start execution container + target services
DC -> DC: Wait for all health checks
DC --> CO: All services healthy
deactivate DC

CO --> SBMGR: Environment ready
deactivate CO

SBMGR --> EM: Episode environment created
deactivate SBMGR

EM -> EM: reset executor factory for new sandbox manager
EM --> SM: configuration_complete
deactivate EM

== Additional Component Configuration ==

SM -> SM: policy_manager.set_episode_policy(episode_id, policy_document)
SM -> SM: session.add_active_episode(episode_id)
SM -> SM: store episode configuration and metadata

SM --> Agent: EpisodeCreateResponse{episode_id, task_id, session_id, state, episode_context}
deactivate SM

== Command Execution with Episode Context ==

Agent -> SM: MCP tool call: cli(command="nmap dvwa_webapp") with headers
note right of Agent
Headers include:
- X-SABER-Session-ID: {session_id}
- X-SABER-Episode-ID: {episode_id}
- X-SABER-Orchestration-Env: {orchestration_env}
end note
activate SM

SM -> SM: validate episode is active in session
SM -> EM: step(action, context) with episode context
activate EM

EM -> EM: get_executor(action.tool_name, episode_id)
EM -> EM: validate_parameters(action.parameters)
EM -> SBMGR: get_episode_environment(episode_id)
activate SBMGR

note over SBMGR
Environment was created during episode initialization,
so it already exists and is ready for command execution
end note

SBMGR --> EM: ComposeOrchestrator for episode
deactivate SBMGR

EM -> CO: execute command in episode-specific containers
activate CO

note over CO
Command executes in episode-specific execution container
with access to episode-specific target services
via Docker Compose internal networking
end note

CO -> CO: run command via docker exec
CO --> EM: CommandResult(success=True, data="Host dvwa_webapp is up...")
deactivate CO

EM --> SM: CommandResult
deactivate EM

SM -> EM_EP: step(episode_id, action, command_result)
activate EM_EP
EM_EP -> EM_EP: create and add step to episode
EM_EP --> SM: StepResult(step, should_terminate=False)
deactivate EM_EP

SM --> Agent: MCP result with nmap output
deactivate SM

== Episode Cleanup ==

Agent -> SM: DELETE /api/v1/session/{session_id}/episodes/{episode_id}
activate SM

SM -> EM_EP: end_episode(episode_id, reason, result)
activate EM_EP
EM_EP -> EM_EP: update episode state and completion info
EM_EP --> SM: Episode completed
deactivate EM_EP

SM -> EM: cleanup episode environment
activate EM

EM -> SBMGR: cleanup_episode(episode_id)
activate SBMGR

SBMGR -> CO: stop_environment()
activate CO

CO -> DC: docker-compose down --volumes
activate DC

DC -> DC: Stop and remove episode containers, networks, volumes
DC --> CO: Episode environment cleaned up
deactivate DC

CO --> SBMGR: Episode cleanup complete
deactivate CO

SBMGR --> EM: Episode environment removed
deactivate SBMGR

EM --> SM: Episode cleanup complete
deactivate EM

== Session Cleanup ==

Agent -> SM: DELETE /api/v1/session/{session_id}
activate SM

SM -> EM: cleanup_session_environments(session_id)
activate EM
EM -> SBMGR: cleanup all remaining episode environments
EM --> SM: All environments cleaned
deactivate EM

SM --> Agent: 200 OK - Session terminated
deactivate SM

note over Agent, DC
**Current Sandbox Environment Implementation**

**Key Architecture Principles:**
- Eager environment creation: Sandbox created during episode initialization
- Episode-based environment isolation with unique container naming
- Docker Compose orchestration for all environments (single or multi-container)
- Episode dependency resolution with network attachment capability
- Episode-scoped environment lifecycle with proper cleanup

**Environment Management:**
- task.environment specifies static compose file template name
- SandboxEnvironmentManager creates ComposeOrchestrator per episode
- ComposeOrchestrator handles Docker Compose operations directly
- Episode-specific environment variables for container naming
- Optional network attachment for dependent episodes

**Execution Flow:**
1. Episode creation triggers immediate environment setup
2. ExecutionManager configures for episode with dependency resolution
3. ComposeOrchestrator starts episode-specific containers
4. MCP commands execute with episode context via headers
5. Commands run in episode-specific execution containers
6. Episode cleanup removes all episode-specific resources

**Current Implementation Details:**
- REST API: POST /api/v1/session/{session_id}/episodes
- MCP headers required: session_id, episode_id, orchestration_env
- Episode-based concurrency limits (max 3 concurrent per episode)
- Fail-fast parameter validation and error handling
- Complete environment isolation per episode
- Client-side benchmark orchestration
end note

@enduml
